## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate data-security policy into concrete export controls.

## Key Concepts
- Data minimization and redaction
- RBAC and per-session scopes
- Token-based session gating
- Centralized exporting logs and retention
- IR playbooks and containment steps

## Code Example
```javascript
// Express route sketch: enforce role-based redaction on export
app.post('/exports/statements', authorize(['viewer','exporter']), (req,res)=>{
  const fields = req.user.role==='exporter' ? fullFields : ['date','amount','balance'];
  const csv = generateCSV(data, fields);
  res.setHeader('Content-Type','text/csv');
  res.send(csv);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the export redaction in CI?
- What metrics indicate success of the control?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests data governance and cloud IAM proficiency in regulated fintech environments, focusing on data lake isolation and rapid IR.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC with tenant attributes
- Deny-by-default bucket policies
- Data-centric protection (field-level encryption/tokenization)
- Policy drift detection (IAM Access Analyzer, CloudTrail)
- Incident response for data leakage (IR playbook, forensics)

## Code Example
```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "*",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant_id": "{tenant_id}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the policy in dev/stage? 
- What metrics indicate policy drift has been mitigated?

## Why This Is Asked

To assess practical control-plane hardening choices under real-world friction, focusing on mobile app token misuse and multi-tenant isolation.

## Key Concepts

- OAuth2/OIDC security best practices (PKCE, short TTLs)
- Token binding and device fingerprinting
- Token rotation and per-tenant scopes
- Token introspection and revocation
- Observability and IR readiness

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rotate refresh token on use and bind to device and tenant
function onRefresh(token, deviceId, tenant) {
  if (!isValidDevice(token.bindInfo, deviceId, tenant)) throw new Error('invalid');
  const newToken = issueAccessToken(tenant, token.tenant);
  rotateRefreshToken(token.id);
  return { accessToken: newToken, refreshToken: getRotatedRefresh(token.id) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the token binding mechanism in CI/CD?
- What metrics indicate token abuse and how would you respond?
